          "heading": "Theoretical Framework and Overview: Time-Varying Fields, Maxwell Equations, and Boundary Conditions",
          "content": "# Unit 1: Maxwell equations and field foundations\n\n## What the unit establishes\n\nStart from the four macroscopic laws, connect integral and differential descriptions, and identify the physical meaning of the displacement current. Students establish an explicit constitutive model before trying to solve time-varying electromagnetic boundary-value problems.\n\n## Reproducible investigation\n\nFor this unit, begin with an oriented loop around a charging capacitor, and identify two surfaces sharing the same boundary. Compute the free conduction current across one and displacement-flux rate across the other; state how their signs relate after orienting the loop. Extend the thought experiment to a capacitor partly filled with a dielectric and ask whether the local displacement-current density is uniform. Next record Gauss, Faraday and Ampere\u2013Maxwell equations in integral and differential forms in one conservation ledger. Mark the location where the material constitutive assumption enters. A smooth numerical plot is not sufficient verification: compare the total free charge change with net boundary current and check that a gauge change preserves E and B. Finally discuss how an ideal capacitor differs from a ceramic device with voltage-dependent capacitance and leakage. The result should be a reproducible model with explicitly labeled approximations.\n\n## Four connected lessons\n\n### 1. Integral Maxwell equations and physical orientation\n\nThe starting point is the physical situation considered in the [full lesson](../lessons/U01-L01-integral-maxwell-equations-and-physical-orientation.html). Its key mathematical relationship is the following, stated with the particular geometrical or material assumptions of this topic:\n\nThe laws are \u222e D\u00b7dA=Q_free, \u222e B\u00b7dA=0, \u222e E\u00b7dl=\u2212d/dt \u222b B\u00b7dA, and \u222e H\u00b7dl=I_free+ d/dt \u222b D\u00b7dA. The time derivatives assume stationary integration paths or require transport terms for moving contours. In a linear homogeneous medium D=\u03b5E and B=\u03bcH, but the field laws do not require those simple linear constitutive models. Apply Gauss to a finite enclosure and Faraday to an oriented loop separately; do not mix an open-surface flux with a closed-surface flux.\n\nWork through its numerical example on paper before using the model. Compare the predicted result with the situation described in the lesson's engineering case; check which assumption restricts the validity of the formula. The goal is not memorization of symbols but identifying whether a simplified scalar parameter is sufficient for this geometry and frequency.\n\n### 2. Differential laws and constitutive material response\n\nThe starting point is the physical situation considered in the [full lesson](../lessons/U01-L02-differential-laws-and-constitutive-material-response.html). Its key mathematical relationship is the following, stated with the particular geometrical or material assumptions of this topic:\n\nThe local forms are \u2207\u00b7D=\u03c1_free, \u2207\u00b7B=0, \u2207\u00d7E=\u2212\u2202B/\u2202t, and \u2207\u00d7H=J_free+\u2202D/\u2202t. For isotropic Ohmic material J=\u03c3E. For a simple dielectric D=\u03b5E; for anisotropic crystal \u03b5 is a tensor and D need not be parallel to E. At discontinuities these derivative formulas become distributional statements or boundary jump conditions; blindly applying one smooth derivative across a sharp interface is unsafe.\n\nWork through its numerical example on paper before using the model. Compare the predicted result with the situation described in the lesson's engineering case; check which assumption restricts the validity of the formula. The goal is not memorization of symbols but identifying whether a simplified scalar parameter is sufficient for this geometry and frequency.\n\n### 3. Charge continuity and displacement current\n\nThe starting point is the physical situation considered in the [full lesson](../lessons/U01-L03-charge-continuity-and-displacement-current.html). Its key mathematical relationship is the following, stated with the particular geometrical or material assumptions of this topic:\n\nThe conservation statement is \u2202\u03c1_free/\u2202t+\u2207\u00b7J_free=0. Since \u2207\u00b7(\u2207\u00d7H)=0, the full Maxwell equation implies \u2207\u00b7J_free+\u2202(\u2207\u00b7D)/\u2202t=0. For ideal parallel plates C=\u03b5A/d and I_C=C dV/dt=\u222b(\u2202D/\u2202t)\u00b7dA. Removing displacement current would wrongly predict contradictory H circulation for the two surfaces sharing one contour.\n\nWork through its numerical example on paper before using the model. Compare the predicted result with the situation described in the lesson's engineering case; check which assumption restricts the validity of the formula. The goal is not memorization of symbols but identifying whether a simplified scalar parameter is sufficient for this geometry and frequency.\n\n### 4. Potentials, gauges and boundary-value completeness\n\nThe starting point is the physical situation considered in the [full lesson](../lessons/U01-L04-potentials-gauges-and-boundary-value-completeness.html). Its key mathematical relationship is the following, stated with the particular geometrical or material assumptions of this topic:\n\nDefine B=\u2207\u00d7A and E=\u2212\u2207\u03c6\u2212\u2202A/\u2202t. Under A\u2032=A+\u2207\u03c7 and \u03c6\u2032=\u03c6\u2212\u2202\u03c7/\u2202t, E and B stay invariant. In Lorenz gauge, \u2207\u00b7A+\u03bc\u03b5\u2202\u03c6/\u2202t=0 for a homogeneous simple material, scalar and vector potentials satisfy wave-type equations with sources. Coulomb gauge \u2207\u00b7A=0 may be more convenient for other boundary setups. Be explicit about temporal sign conventions when converting to phasors.\n\nWork through its numerical example on paper before using the model. Compare the predicted result with the situation described in the lesson's engineering case; check which assumption restricts the validity of the formula. The goal is not memorization of symbols but identifying whether a simplified scalar parameter is sufficient for this geometry and frequency.\n\n## Unit test of understanding\n\nUse the [eight solved questions](../assessments/unit-01.html) as a closed-book audit. Then complete the [unit investigation](../projects/unit-01.html) and compare its computational trend with the [unit interactive model](../simulations/model-01.html). Write down any mismatch between the ideal analytical limit and the plotted values; if an output appears to violate conservation or dimensional consistency, diagnose the implementation before adjusting a physical parameter to force agreement.\n\n## Evidence standard\n\nA completed study record contains the source equations, declared time and orientation convention, parameter units, at least two numerical cases, a model-limitation statement and a reproducible test. Nothing in this unit requires live mains or high-voltage apparatus; synthetic numerical work is adequate for the computational project. A credible claim about real equipment would additionally require calibration and qualified verification appropriate to that application.\n"
