          "secNumber": "5.1",
          "heading": "Theoretical Framework and Overview: Transmission Line Theory, Transient Reflections, and Smith Chart",
          "content": "# Unit 5: Waveguides, resonators and planar propagation\n\n## What the unit establishes\n\nRelate electromagnetic confinement to cutoff, dispersion, stored energy, loss and conductor geometry; compare hollow metallic guides with PCB transmission structures.\n\n## Reproducible investigation\n\nChoose a rectangular waveguide and calculate TE10 cutoff for three broad-wall dimensions, keeping the dielectric fixed. At an operating frequency above cutoff, determine \u03b2, guide wavelength, group velocity and phase velocity. Explain why phase speed exceeding c is not a causality violation. Move frequency gradually toward cutoff and identify how a narrowband pulse might become delayed and distorted. Sketch the transverse field structure relative to the conducting walls; state why TEM is not supported in the ideal hollow single-conductor geometry. Then define a cavity with a loaded resonance and compute Q from bandwidth and approximate stored-energy decay. Compare this with a PCB microstrip whose effective dielectric constant and characteristic impedance are governed by fringing fields rather than a hollow guide's cutoff equation.\n\n## Four connected lessons\n\n### 1. Hollow conducting waveguide and modal cutoff\n\nThe starting point is the physical situation considered in the [full lesson](../lessons/U05-L01-hollow-conducting-waveguide-and-modal-cutoff.html). Its key mathematical relationship is the following, stated with the particular geometrical or material assumptions of this topic:\n\nFor an ideal rectangular guide filled with dielectric \u03b5,\u03bc, the cutoff wavenumber is kc=\u03c0sqrt[(m/a)\u00b2+(n/b)\u00b2] and fc=(1/(2sqrt(\u03bc\u03b5)))sqrt[(m/a)\u00b2+(n/b)\u00b2]. For TE10, fc=v/(2a). Above cutoff, \u03b2=sqrt[(\u03c9sqrt(\u03bc\u03b5))\u00b2\u2212kc\u00b2]. The cutoffs come from satisfying conductor boundary conditions, not from the transmission-line quarter-wave formula.\n\nWork through its numerical example on paper before using the model. Compare the predicted result with the situation described in the lesson's engineering case; check which assumption restricts the validity of the formula. The goal is not memorization of symbols but identifying whether a simplified scalar parameter is sufficient for this geometry and frequency.\n\n### 2. Guide phase velocity, group velocity and dispersion\n\nThe starting point is the physical situation considered in the [full lesson](../lessons/U05-L02-guide-phase-velocity-group-velocity-and-dispersion.html). Its key mathematical relationship is the following, stated with the particular geometrical or material assumptions of this topic:\n\nFor a lossless hollow guide, \u03b2=k sqrt[1\u2212(fc/f)\u00b2], v_phase=v/sqrt[1\u2212(fc/f)\u00b2], and v_group=v sqrt[1\u2212(fc/f)\u00b2]. Guided wavelength \u03bb_g=2\u03c0/\u03b2=\u03bb/sqrt[1\u2212(fc/f)\u00b2]. For TE modes the wave impedance is Z_TE=\u03c9\u03bc/\u03b2; it differs from both intrinsic impedance and an arbitrary circuit reference impedance.\n\nWork through its numerical example on paper before using the model. Compare the predicted result with the situation described in the lesson's engineering case; check which assumption restricts the validity of the formula. The goal is not memorization of symbols but identifying whether a simplified scalar parameter is sufficient for this geometry and frequency.\n\n### 3. Cavity resonators, energy storage and quality factor\n\nThe starting point is the physical situation considered in the [full lesson](../lessons/U05-L03-cavity-resonators-energy-storage-and-quality-factor.html). Its key mathematical relationship is the following, stated with the particular geometrical or material assumptions of this topic:\n\nDefine Q=\u03c90 U/P_loss for steady-state stored energy U and average dissipated power P_loss. For a weakly damped resonator, energy decay has a time constant \u03c4_E\u2248Q/\u03c90 in the unloaded one-pole model. The approximate 3 dB bandwidth is \u0394f\u2248f0/Q_loaded for a well-isolated resonance. For parallel independent loss mechanisms, 1/Q_total\u2248\u03a3 1/Q_i; port coupling contributes its own loss term.\n\nWork through its numerical example on paper before using the model. Compare the predicted result with the situation described in the lesson's engineering case; check which assumption restricts the validity of the formula. The goal is not memorization of symbols but identifying whether a simplified scalar parameter is sufficient for this geometry and frequency.\n\n### 4. Microstrip fields, effective permittivity and dispersion\n\nThe starting point is the physical situation considered in the [full lesson](../lessons/U05-L04-microstrip-fields-effective-permittivity-and-dispersion.html). Its key mathematical relationship is the following, stated with the particular geometrical or material assumptions of this topic:\n\nA common thin-trace empirical approximation for w/h\u22641 is Z0\u2248(60/sqrt(\u03b5_eff)) ln(8h/w+w/(4h)), and \u03b5_eff\u2248(\u03b5_r+1)/2+(\u03b5_r\u22121)/(2sqrt(1+12h/w)). These are engineering approximations with limited thickness and frequency validity, not exact Maxwell solutions. The guided phase velocity is approximately c/sqrt(\u03b5_eff), and physical quarter-wave length is c/(4f sqrt(\u03b5_eff)).\n\nWork through its numerical example on paper before using the model. Compare the predicted result with the situation described in the lesson's engineering case; check which assumption restricts the validity of the formula. The goal is not memorization of symbols but identifying whether a simplified scalar parameter is sufficient for this geometry and frequency.\n\n## Unit test of understanding\n\nUse the [eight solved questions](../assessments/unit-05.html) as a closed-book audit. Then complete the [unit investigation](../projects/unit-05.html) and compare its computational trend with the [unit interactive model](../simulations/model-05.html). Write down any mismatch between the ideal analytical limit and the plotted values; if an output appears to violate conservation or dimensional consistency, diagnose the implementation before adjusting a physical parameter to force agreement.\n\n## Evidence standard\n\nA completed study record contains the source equations, declared time and orientation convention, parameter units, at least two numerical cases, a model-limitation statement and a reproducible test. Nothing in this unit requires live mains or high-voltage apparatus; synthetic numerical work is adequate for the computational project. A credible claim about real equipment would additionally require calibration and qualified verification appropriate to that application.\n"
